Day 43/100 of #DrGVishwanathanChallenge
Today's focus was on solving Heap (Priority Queue) based problems, improving my understanding of greedy strategies and efficient data structures.
 Problem 1: 373. Find K Pairs with Smallest Sums (Medium)
 Approach:
Used a Min Heap (Priority Queue) to always retrieve the pair having the smallest sum.
Initially inserted pairs formed by every element of nums1 with the first element of nums2.
Repeatedly extracted the minimum pair, added it to the answer, and inserted the next possible pair from nums2.
This avoids generating all possible pairs, making the solution much more efficient.

Time Complexity:O((n + k) log n),n = nums1.length
Space Complexity:O(n)

Problem 2: 1046. Last Stone Weight (Easy)
Approach:
Stored all stones in a Max Heap.
Repeatedly removed the two heaviest stones.
If their weights were different, inserted the remaining weight back into the heap.
Continued until one or no stone remained.

Time Complexity: O(n log n)
Space Complexity: O(n)

 Key Learnings
1) Efficient use of Priority Queues (Min Heap & Max Heap).
2) Optimizing pair generation instead of brute force.
3) Applying greedy techniques with heaps for faster solutions.
